🔗 LINKER
==========

📘 What is a Linker?
--------------------
A linker is a system program that combines multiple object files (.o) and libraries (.a / .so) into a final executable file.

It:
- Resolves symbol references (e.g., function calls, global variables) between modules.
- Allows modular programming — you don’t need all code in one file.

Without a linker, every program would need to contain all its code in a single file.


⚙️ WHAT IS LINKING?
--------------------
Linking is the process of combining object code and libraries to produce an executable.

- Performed by a linker (link editor)
- It’s the last step in the compilation process
- Can be done in two ways:
  1. Copying the library code into the executable (Static Linking)
  2. Referring to the library code that is loaded at runtime (Dynamic Linking)


🧩 TYPES OF LINKING
====================

1️ STATIC LINKING
-------------------
- Uses Static Libraries (.a)
- The linker copies all used library functions into the executable file.
- The final executable is self-contained (does not depend on external libraries).

✅ Advantages:
- No need for libraries at runtime.
- Faster execution (everything included in the binary).
- Portable — can run anywhere.

❌ Disadvantages:
- Larger executable size.
- Must recompile if the library is updated.

2️ DYNAMIC LINKING
--------------------
- Uses Shared Libraries (.so)
- The linker does not copy the library code.
- Instead, it stores references to the shared library.
- The shared library is loaded into memory at runtime.

✅ Advantages:
- Smaller executable size.
- Multiple programs can share the same library in memory.
- Easier to update — updating the library updates all dependent programs.

❌ Disadvantages:
- Library must be present at runtime.
- Slightly slower startup (libraries are loaded dynamically).



